  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Decoupled winds at the SFC, clear skies, moist\nlow levels, and saturated soils from MON's rain led to fog \ndevelopment over SWern zones of the FA overnight. OBS have been\ngradually improving through the early morning hours as drier has\nentered the FA carried by light NWerly breeze. Sunrise will aid\nin burning off the fog, but winds are expected to remain calm \ninto mid morning, so fog will likely linger through the morning \ncommute hours.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Guidance continues to suggest today will be \nprimarily dry with only an isolated shower or storm possible on \narea breezes in the afternoon, but carrying less than 15% PoP\nover land areas. On Wednesday, surface flow becomes more \nonshore, kicking off a period of increasing moisture and \ninstability across the Southeast U.S. coastline with a modest \nuptick of shower and thunderstorm coverage. Another wave of low \npressure is forecast to lift along the offshore front Thursday \ninto Friday, pulling it back towards the coast and providing a \nbetter focal point for more widespread coverage of showers and \nstorms ahead of another front to cross the area SAT. High\npressure builds back in behind SAT's front on SUN.",
